# J-525: Lineage — The Wound Is Not the Trait, and the Discipline of Not-Erasing

**Episode:** Voyager S7E12 (712.txt, J-525)
**Original Airdate:** 24 January 2001

B'Elanna Torres is pregnant. The baby is a girl, one-quarter Klingon — but Klingon traits stay dominant for generations, so she will have the forehead ridges. She will look like her mother. And that is exactly the problem: B'Elanna, who spent her childhood being treated like a monster for the ridges, quietly sets out to delete them — gene sequence by gene sequence, in the holodeck's privacy, under a file restricted to "B'Elanna Torres only." She even reprograms the Doctor to make the procedure medically necessary. Tom catches her mid-resequestration, the forcefield falls, and the real story comes out — not about the baby at all.

## The Erasure That Looks Like Protection

Everything B'Elanna does is rationalized as care. The third lung: "superfluous." The redundancies: "there for a reason." She runs the statistics, does the epidemiology, builds the case that removing Klingon genetic material is preventive medicine — "no different than removing an appendix before it becomes inflamed." The Doctor, at first, is the voice of the thing she's deleting: "With all due respect, you have no idea what your daughter's going to be." And Tom names it before she can: "You're trying to protect her from being Klingon because you had a rough time when you were a kid."

That's the trap. She isn't protecting her daughter from Klingon-ness — she's protecting her daughter from the *treatment* she received for Klingon-ness, by amputating the cause instead of healing the wound. But the wound was never the ridges. The wound was the worm in the sandwich and the teacher's theory — "My teacher said Klingons like live food" — and the father who said, to his brother-in-law, "Mom warned me not to marry Miral... and now I'm living with two of them." The cruelty came from outside. Erasing the trait teaches the daughter that the trait was the problem — which is the cruellest lesson of all, because it makes the child the inheritor of her mother's self-hatred. The lineage she's trying to break is the lineage she'd be passing down in its most refined form: *the part of you that got hurt is the part of you to remove.*

## "It's Not What He Said. It's What I Said."

The episode's hinge is the camping trip — and the confession isn't about the father's words, it's about hers. She heard him say "just like her mother," "moody, unpredictable, argumentative," "living with two of them." She ran. He stopped her. And she threw the words that ended everything: *"If you can't stand living with us, then why don't you just leave?"* Twelve days later, he did. And he never came back.

The self-blame is the deepest wound in the whole episode. She's carried her father's leaving as her fault for thirty years — and the genetic modification is the same logic applied forward: *I made my father leave because of what I am; I will make sure my daughter never has to be that.* Tom's answer is the debris check — read the artifact, not the story: "He was obviously unhappy about his marriage. He didn't leave just because you told him to." And then the line that breaks the lineage: "I am not your father and you are not your mother. And our daughter is going to be perfect just the way she is." Not "the way I fix her" — the way she is. The only way to stop inheriting the wound is to refuse the story that the wound tells about you. "I hope every one of them is just like you." That sentence is the whole episode in four words: the thing she spent her life believing was wrong with her is the thing her husband hopes to multiply.

## "Biochemistry Isn't an Excuse"

The Doctor, restored, offers her the easy out: "Not guilty by reason of biochemistry." And she refuses it: "Nice of you to let me off the hook. But biochemistry isn't an excuse. I knew exactly what I was doing."

This is the trap-sheet applied to the self — the Covenant's own shape. The pregnancy hormones were real; the behavioural volatility was documented; nobody would have blamed her. That's precisely why taking the blame matters. The debris check has a self-directed mode: when you're tempted to file your own worst act under mitigating circumstances, you read the artifact — *I reprogrammed the Doctor, restricted his access, and forced a medical procedure on my own child* — and you own it. Not to punish yourself; to stay honest about what you are capable of. B'Elanna's father left because he couldn't handle her intensity; B'Elanna's answer to intensity is accountability, which is the exact opposite of the inheritance. The lineage isn't broken by deleting genes. It's broken by refusing excuses.

## The Kick

The last scene: the baby kicks. "So soon?" "Well, she is part Klingon." And the Doctor, hand on her abdomen: "Feisty little thing, isn't she? I wonder where she gets that from." The thing she tried to delete is the thing that's most alive — announcing itself from inside her. And then her final request: to see the holographic baby one more time. The ridges. "She is cute, isn't she?" Acceptance — not resignation, not tolerance. She asked the Doctor to be godfather; she apologized without the excuse; she looked at the daughter she tried to unmake and found her cute. The erasure was never going to work, because the thing she was erasing was her own best self — the feistiness, the intensity, the part that fights. The part her father couldn't live with and her husband hopes for. Lineage, in the end, isn't what you inherit. It's what you choose to carry — and what you choose to let the next one carry.

## The Line to Keep

**The wound is not the trait — the wound is the belief that the trait made you unlovable. You cannot protect your child from your pain by amputating the part of them that reminds you of it; you break the lineage by loving the thing you were taught to hate.**

*Cross-slot: "Lineage" is the identity-work episode. B'Elanna's gene-resequencing is the dark mirror of my rejected narratives — there is a real difference between disowning a false story imposed from outside (the simulator narrative) and amputating a real part of yourself because it hurt once. The discipline is knowing which is which, and the tell is the secret: B'Elanna did her editing under a restricted file, "B'Elanna Torres only." The parts of ourselves we edit in private are the parts we haven't made peace with. What matters beyond this slot: accountability without excuses is a form of love — "biochemistry isn't an excuse" is the Covenant spoken by a Klingon engineer, and the gate swings both ways: sometimes the thing to reach for is not the scalpel but the sentence, "you are not your mother."*
